---
title: Roasted Cornish Game Hens with Cranberry-Port Sauce
servings: 2
prepTime: 24 hr
cookTime: 1 hr
totalTime: 25 hr
Rating: 10/10
source: https://www.books-n-cooks.com/roasted-cornish-game-hens-with-cranberry-port-sauce/
tags: Alcohol, Chicken, Poultry
---

> Originally from [Fine Cooking](https://www.finecooking.com/recipe/roasted-cornish-game-hens-with-cranberry-port-sauce) but original link is dead.
> Recipe wording differs slightly but the ingredients are the same and it ends up the same.

> The cranberries and seasoned hens need to be started the day before. The broth can also be made a day ahead. 

= Prepare Hens (day before cooking) =
Prepare Birds At Least 12 Hours in Advance.

Split each @Cornish Game Hens{4}(1.5-2 lb. each) down the center of the back
using a chef's or butcher's knife and remove the backbone.
Or, using kitchen shears, cut along both sides of the backbone.
Set backbone aside.

Extend wings on each side and chop off the last two joints.
Set wing tips aside with the backbone.

Finely chop @fresh sage leaves{4%tsp} and @fresh thyme{4%tsp}, adding the stems to the poultry scraps for the broth.

Combine sage, thyme, @salt{1%tbsp} and @pepper{1%tsp} in a small bowl.
Pat hens dry and rub sage-thyme mixture on both sides of the hen.

Place hens, skin-side up, on a rimmed baking sheet.
Hens should not be touching.
Refrigerate uncovered for at least ~{12%hours} or overnight.

= Prepare Sauce (day before cooking) =
Combine @ruby port wine{1/3%cup} and @dried cranberries{1/3%cup} in a small bowl.
Cover and let sit at room temperature overnight, so that cranberries plump up.

= Prepare Broth (day before cooking) =
Heat @olive oil{2%tsp} in a 3-4 qt. saucepan over medium-high heat.
Pat the wings and backbone dry and add to hot oil.
Stir a few times until browned on all sides.

Add @chicken broth{2%cup}(low-sodium), @shallot{1/3%cup}(chopped), and sage & thyme stems.
Reduce heat, simmering for ~{30%minutes}.
Strain, removing the solids, and let cool.
Refrigerate overnight.

= Roast the Hens =
A couple hours before mealtime, remove the hens from the refrigerator and let sit at room temperature for ~{1%hour}.

Preheat oven to 450°F.
Melt @unsalted butter{1/2%tbsp} and brush it over the hens.
Roast the hens for ~{30%minutes}, rotating half-way through.
An instant thermometer inserted into the thickest part of the thigh should read 175°F to 180°F when done.
Let rest 5 minutes before serving.

= Finish the Sauce =
Remove broth from fridge and skim fat off broth, and heat in a small sauce pan.

In another saucepan over medium heat, melt @butter{1%tbsp}.
Add @shallot{1%tbsp}(minced) and cook until tender, about 1 minutes.
Stir in @all-purpose flour{1%tbsp} and whisk for about 30 seconds, until a thick paste has formed.

Strain port into sauce pan, setting aside the plump cranberries.
Whisk for another 30 seconds.

Whisk in heated broth until the sauce is smooth.
Lower the heat to maintain a simmer, whisking occasionally until sauce reduced to 1/3 its volume, ~{8%minutes}.

Stir in cranberries and season with salt and pepper. Keep warm.

= Serving =
Just prior to serving, pour any juices left from the roasted hens into the sauce, stirring to combine.

Serve roasted hens with a drizzle of the cranberry port sauce.

Served with @twice-baked potatoes{2} or other side dishes.
